1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:05,919
This is a tiny GPT reading a line of Shakespeare.

2
00:00:06,198 --> 00:00:10,782
And this grid shows where each of its attention heads is looking while it reads.

3
00:00:11,062 --> 00:00:15,119
Look at this one: a perfect diagonal, just under the main one.

4
00:00:15,399 --> 00:00:18,639
Every character is looking at the character right before it.

5
00:00:18,919 --> 00:00:21,847
And this head looks exactly two characters back.

6
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:23,999
Nobody programmed that.

7
00:00:24,279 --> 00:00:27,855
The model worked it out by itself from predicting the next letter.

8
00:00:28,135 --> 00:00:37,087
In this video I'll show you how I pulled these maps out of a real transformer, what each layer turns out to do, and where this kind of picture stops being trustworthy.

9
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:41,711
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

10
00:00:42,511 --> 00:00:49,135
You've probably seen the famous diagram: a sentence, with lines of different thickness connecting the words.

11
00:00:49,415 --> 00:00:51,287
That's attention.

12
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:56,559
But most of those pictures are drawn by hand, or from huge models you can't run yourself.

13
00:00:56,839 --> 00:01:03,631
Here the model is small enough to train in a few minutes on a laptop CPU, and we can look at every single head.

14
00:01:03,911 --> 00:01:14,639
It's the same mechanism as the big models: every character produces a query, every earlier character offers a key, and the match between them decides how much information flows.

15
00:01:14,919 --> 00:01:19,983
The heatmap is just that matching score, after softmax, for every pair.

16
00:01:20,263 --> 00:01:25,663
One thing that makes it causal: a character may only look backwards, never forwards.

17
00:01:25,943 --> 00:01:28,343
That's why every map is a triangle.

18
00:01:29,143 --> 00:01:34,591
This project reuses the model from project seven, so there is no new architecture.

19
00:01:34,871 --> 00:01:41,711
Its attention layer is PyTorch's MultiheadAttention, and by default the model asks it not to return the weights.

20
00:01:41,991 --> 00:01:45,855
To look inside, I run the blocks myself and ask for them..

21
00:01:46,135 --> 00:01:57,919
It's the same forward pass as before, with two changes: need_weights is true, and average_attn_weights is false, so I get one map per head instead of one averaged map.

22
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:02,999
For each of the four layers I keep an array of heads by positions by positions.

23
00:02:03,279 --> 00:02:08,895
At the end, the same head layer gives me the probability of every possible next character.

24
00:02:09,175 --> 00:02:19,711
The model itself was trained a bit longer than in project seven: two thousand steps with a decaying learning rate, saved to a three megabyte file, so the app loads instantly.

25
00:02:20,511 --> 00:02:22,383
Layer one.

26
00:02:22,663 --> 00:02:24,895
Head two: a sharp diagonal.

27
00:02:25,175 --> 00:02:32,735
Averaged over two hundred random windows of Shakespeare, it puts ninety-nine percent of its attention on the previous character.

28
00:02:33,015 --> 00:02:37,647
Head one does the same trick, two characters back, ninety-seven percent.

29
00:02:37,927 --> 00:02:46,591
Heads three and four are looser: they mostly look one back, fifty-nine and seventy-eight percent, and spread the rest over earlier text.

30
00:02:46,871 --> 00:02:56,519
So the very first thing this transformer learned is to build its own short window: for every position, it copies in what the one or two previous characters were.

31
00:02:56,799 --> 00:03:01,119
That's exactly the information a next-character predictor needs first.

32
00:03:01,399 --> 00:03:03,366
Layer two is less tidy.

33
00:03:03,646 --> 00:03:09,598
Several heads still lean on the previous character, but they spread weight over the last few positions.

34
00:03:09,878 --> 00:03:19,119
By layer four, the simple diagonals are gone, and some maps show bright vertical stripes: many characters looking at the same few earlier places.

35
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:25,663
No head puts more than about twenty-one percent on itself, the previous character, or the one before that.

36
00:03:25,943 --> 00:03:27,815
That doesn't mean they're useless.

37
00:03:28,095 --> 00:03:34,646
It means they're doing something that isn't a simple offset, and a heatmap can't tell me what those places have in common.

38
00:03:35,447 --> 00:03:38,591
Below the map there's a slider for a character position.

39
00:03:38,871 --> 00:03:45,039
For that character I show how much it attends to each earlier one, and what the model predicts comes next.

40
00:03:45,319 --> 00:03:50,503
After "ROMEO:", the model predicts a newline with one hundred percent probability.

41
00:03:50,783 --> 00:03:53,927
It has seen that speaker format thousands of times.

42
00:03:54,207 --> 00:03:59,511
After "doth teac", it predicts the letter h, again at ninety-nine percent.

43
00:03:59,791 --> 00:04:02,383
That's the model finishing a word it knows.

44
00:04:02,663 --> 00:04:04,895
At other places it's honestly unsure.

45
00:04:05,175 --> 00:04:13,959
After "he doth te", it says l at thirty-six percent and a at thirty-three percent: the real word, teach, starts with the second one.

46
00:04:14,239 --> 00:04:18,991
This is a small model, and uncertainty like that is normal and visible.

47
00:04:19,791 --> 00:04:21,663
Now the part that matters.

48
00:04:21,943 --> 00:04:25,303
An attention map is a clue, not an explanation.

49
00:04:25,583 --> 00:04:32,614
Later layers mix information from every head, and the weights don't tell you how much a value actually changes the output.

50
00:04:32,895 --> 00:04:37,143
A head that attends strongly to a character may be doing little with it.

51
00:04:37,423 --> 00:04:43,903
The numbers here are averages over a modest sample of Shakespeare windows, and one trained model with one seed.

52
00:04:44,183 --> 00:04:46,895
A different training run would have different heads.

53
00:04:47,175 --> 00:04:53,295
And the model only knows characters from the training text, so unusual characters get skipped in the app.

54
00:04:53,575 --> 00:05:01,063
I built this with Claude Code, and I ran the measurements myself, but please treat the percentages as examples from this one model.

55
00:05:01,862 --> 00:05:07,502
Next I'd like to track how these heads appear during training, so you can watch the previous-character head form.

56
00:05:07,782 --> 00:05:15,847
I'd also like to try knocking one head out and measuring how much worse the predictions get, which is a much better test than just looking at a heatmap.

57
00:05:16,126 --> 00:05:18,119
The code is in the description.

58
00:05:18,398 --> 00:05:20,798
No API key, no cost.

59
00:05:21,078 --> 00:05:28,110
If you enjoyed looking inside a GPT, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which head to investigate next.
